cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this result, these proofs or these tools, please cite them as below. Text and figures: CC BY 4.0. Code (theory/ and probe/): MIT."
title: "Local maxima of the moment-map norm on a unitary orbit: a root-block sign test, mutual information, and Klyachko's cubicles"
authors:
  - name: "Moki & Julio"
version: "1.0"
doi: "{{DOI}}"
date-released: "2026-10-10"
repository-code: "https://github.com/Hashirama1337s/moment-map-local-maxima"
license: CC-BY-4.0
keywords:
  - moment map
  - norm square of the moment map
  - Kirwan-Ness
  - local maxima
  - unitary orbit
  - quantum mereology
  - tensor product structure
  - minimal interaction
  - mutual information
  - Klyachko cubicles
  - standard Young tableaux
  - coherent Boolean term orders
  - Farkas certificate
  - McConnell-Jennings counts
abstract: >-
  Let H be a Hermitian matrix with simple spectrum, K a compact connected subgroup of U(d) acting on the unitary orbit
  of H by conjugation, and P the orthogonal projection onto the Hermitian part of Lie(K) plus the scalars. Minimising the
  minimal-interaction cost ||H' - P(H')||^2 over the orbit is maximising ||P(H')||^2, the squared norm of the moment map.
  Critical points satisfy [P(H'), H'] = 0 (Kirwan, Ness); at a local maximum the K-orbit is symplectic and the stabiliser
  of the point equals that of its moment-map image (Biliotti); the one-line consequence that the point is fixed by a
  maximal torus T is recorded here. We prove that a T-fixed point D is a local maximum if and only if P(D) orders the
  eigenvalues of D strictly as D does across distinct T-weight spaces, and that it is then a strict local maximum modulo
  an explicit critical set (the K-orbit of D when the weights are multiplicity-free). The proof is a root-by-root Hessian
  analysis with a variance identity, an exact tie formula and a slice argument. The same test, with P(D) replaced by the
  gradient of F, holds for every C^2 K-invariant functional F(P(H')) with positive-definite second derivative, including
  minus the sum of the marginal entropies. For two parties this gives the local minima of mutual information on the
  unitary orbit of a full-rank state with simple spectrum: the classical states whose probabilities are ordered like the
  product of their own marginals. Those orders are Klyachko's cubicles (realizable standard Young tableaux). Every cubicle is the
  unique global-minimiser class on a nonempty open set of spectra, for both functionals; combined with the classical
  global minimum of mutual information (Jevtic, Jennings, Rudolph), this identifies McConnell and Jennings's numerical
  class counts 1, 5, 14, 42, 18 (shapes 2x2, 2x3, 2x4, 2x5, 3x3) with cubicle counts. The six non-realizable 3x3 tableaux
  carry exact Farkas certificates. Degenerate spectra and the sharpness of the single-spectrum bounds are left open.
  The repository also contains the exact algorithms, independent implementations and the full numerical ledger.
